# Introduction

@<introduction of litar@=
Programmers have a lot of text to deal with. They write code,
they write code to compile code, they write document, they write
code to format document, they write code to parse whatever text,
they even spell commands to control computer through shells. Basically,
all work that programmers do is writing text.

The traditional unit of text is file, text of different types
is saved in seperated files, but sometimes, it might take
too much energy to create a new file. Like, you want to
insert a build time information to your code whenever you build
your program. To do that, you might write a simple sed script and
put that to Makefile. Here is the problem, sed operate on the whole
file, so you have to make sure that the sed script won't influence the other
part of the file. To solve that, you might seperate that part of code to a new file,
use a function to encapsulate it or simply store the information
in a macro or a variable to pass back to the original file.
You can see all the extra efforts made here.

litar is created to operate on a smaller unit of text, which is called chunk.
With litar, you can gather text of whatever type to a single file
and describe the relations between chunks at ease. Some relations
are hard or cumbersome to implement if between files.
What's more, follow the spirit of literate programming,
the readability is supposed to be improved a lot.
A such file is called litar archive, the name litar is from
literate and archive.
@

@<syntax of litar@=
Because a litar archive is supposed to contain text of all types, the syntax of
itself should be simple. The syntax of a litar archive is built upon
control characters, which are @@ followed by a character.

To fully describe all concepts, we shall introduce another term, block.
In the outermost, there are blocks interleaved with comments.
Each block consists of two parts: the name of a chunk and contents of current block.

```
Some comments.

@@<chunk1@@=
The contents of block1.
@@

Some other comments

@@<chunk2@@=
The contents of block2.
@@

@@<chunk1@@=
The contents of block3.
@@

```

We say here that chunk1 is extended by block1 and block3, chunk2 is extended by block2.

There is another relation between chunk and block, include.

```
Some comments.

@@<chunk1@@=
The contents of block1.
@@

Some other comments

@@<chunk2@@=
The contents of block2.
@@<chunk1@@>
The contents of block2.
@@

```

The contents of block2 contains a reference to chunk1, we say block2 includes chunk1
at the position of the reference to chunk1.

The definition for the content of a chunk is the expanded contents of all
blocks appended to the chunk concatenated.
The definition for the expanded contents of a block is the content of the block
that all references to chunk are replaced by the content of the corresponding chunk.

It's a recursive procedure to evaluate the content of a chunk.
So it's wrong if a block includes the chunk that it extends.

The syntax above decribes some common functionalities all literate programming tools have.
litar has some useful extensions, which make it a general purpose tool
for text processing rather than a literate programming tool.
As a result litar will not take in weaving to its kernel.

The major extension is user defined pipeline.
Noweb has some similar design decisions, but litar makes use of pipeline even more.

The following simple program explains the usage of pipeline in litar.

```
@@<hello.c@@=
#include "stdio.h"
int main() {
    @@<print hello world@@|exaggeratedly@@>
    return 0;
}
@@

@@<print hello world@@=
printf("Hello, world!\n");
@@

@@<exaggeratedly@@=
sed 's/world/WORLD/'
@@

```

The final output of this program is "Hello, WORLD!". The creation
of a chunk is pretty easy compared to files, so is the operation to
the chunk. The operation take effect through a method like Unix pipeline,
and itself is nothing more than a chunk.

Operations are bash scripts by default, litar may support other languages in future
or simply leave it as an option to users.

Relation include is extended to, a block include a chunk transformed by
filters at the position of reference to the chunk.

The whole design of litar is around this relation, all other stuffs are
supplements to this.

Besides these control characters that describes the structure of a litar archive,
there are some other control characters. They are commands, which influence
the process of dealing with litar archive. For example, @@i receive an argument
of file name, and insert the content of that file at the place of @@i.
@

# Invocation

@<invocation syntax@=
{
    "name": "litar",
    "options": [
        @<command line options for litar@|remove ending comma@>
    ],
    "arguments": [
        "ARCHIVE"
    ]
}
@

@<manpage of litar@=
.TH LITAR 1
.SH NAME
litar \- take action on litar archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
@<invocation syntax@|synopsis for manpage@>
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B litar
interacts with litar archives to extract contents of a chunk or
execute an operation under a context built from archive.
.SH OPTIONS
@<invocation syntax@|options for manpage@>
@

# Implementation

litar follows the common pattern of a command line tool, just executes and exits.

@<litar.c@=
@<includes@>
@<declarations@>
@<datatypes@>
@<flags@>
@<global variables@>
@<functions@>

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    @<initializations of global variables@>
    @<parse command line arguments to set flags and global variables@>
    @<perform operations according to flags@>
    return 0;
}
@

@<functions@=
void print_usage()
{
    printf("Usage: %s\n", @<invocation syntax@|usage of invocation syntax@>);
}
@

The operations of litar can be performed at two stages:

1. before parsing,
2. after parsing input.

Not all invocations need to go through parsing.

@<perform operations according to flags@=
do {
    bool to_continue = true;
    @<operations before parsing@|execute sequentially@>
    if (!to_continue) {
        break;
    }

    @<parse input@>

    @<operations after parsing input@|execute sequentially@>
} while (0);
@

@<global variables@=
char *archive_name = NULL;
@

@<parse command line arguments to set flags and global variables@=
do {
    struct stat buf;
    int opt;

    while (
        (
            opt = getopt(
                argc,
                argv,
                @<invocation syntax@|getopt string for invocation syntax@>
            )
        ) != -1
    ) {
        switch (opt) {
        @<cases for command line option@>
        default:
            print_usage();
            return 1;
        }
    }

    if (optind < argc) {
        archive_name = argv[optind];

        if (access(archive_name, R_OK) == -1) {
            perror(archive_name);
            return 1;
        }
    }
} while (0);
@

@<command line options for litar@=
["Show the usage of litar.", "h"],
@

@<flags@=
bool to_print_help;
@

@<cases for command line option@=
case 'h':
    to_print_help = true;
    break;
@

@<operations before parsing@=
if (to_print_help) {
    print_usage();
    to_continue = false;
}
@

@<command line options for litar@=
["Show the version and build information of litar.", "v"],
@

@<flags@=
bool to_print_version_info;
@

@<cases for command line option@=
case 'v':
    to_print_version_info = true;
    break;
@

@<global variables@=
char version[] = "dev";
@

@<operations before parsing@=
if (to_print_version_info) {
    @<print version information@|fill in build time information@>
    to_continue = false;
    break;
}
@

@<print version information@=
printf("Version %s, built at <build time>\n", version);
@

## Parsing

The purpose of parsing is to extract relations between objects belonging to
a specific domain from unstructured information.

@<functions@=
@<relations between object@>
@

@<declarations@=
typedef struct mappings_chunk *Chunk;
typedef struct mappings_block *Block;
typedef struct mappings_chunk_ref *ChunkRef;
@

@<datatypes@=
struct mappings_chunk {
    @<mappings from chunk@>
};

struct mappings_block {
    @<mappings from block@>
};

struct mappings_chunk_ref {
    @<mappings from chunk ref@>
};
@

To save space, reference to the original text is represented by some records
rather than stored to memory.

@<datatypes@=
typedef struct {
    char *file;
    int start;
    int end;
} *TextRef;
@

Let's begin with chunks. All chunks have a name and
there's only one chunk if given a name. The name of a chunk
consists of none-space words, so "name of chunk" does not
differ from "  name  of  chunk".

@<declarations@=
void normalize_chunk_name(const char *orig, char *norm);
@

@<functions@=
void normalize_chunk_name(const char *orig, char *norm)
{
    int i = 0, j = 0, l = strlen(orig);
    while (i < l) {
        if (isspace(orig[i])) {
            if (j != 0 && norm[j-1] != ' ') {
                norm[j++] = ' ';
            }
        } else {
            norm[j++] = orig[i];
        }
        ++i;
    }
    if (norm[j-1] == ' ') {
        norm[j-1] = '\0';
    }
}
@

@<mappings from chunk@=
char *chunk_name;
@

@<global variables@=
HashTable chunk_name_to_chunk = NULL;
@

@<initializations of global variables@=
new_string_hash_table(&chunk_name_to_chunk);
@

@<relations between object@=
Chunk chunk_of_name(const char *name)
{
    char *norm;
    norm = strdup(name);
    normalize_chunk_name(name, norm);

    Chunk chk;
    chk = get_from_hash_table(chunk_name_to_chunk, norm);
    if (chk == NULL) {
        NEW0(chk);
        put_to_hash_table(chunk_name_to_chunk, norm, chk);
        chk->chunk_name = norm;
    } else {
        free(norm);
    }

    return chk;
}

const char *name_of_chunk(Chunk chk)
{
    return chk->chunk_name;
}
@

Chunk is extended by a list of blocks.

@<mappings from chunk@=
List blocks;
@

@<mappings from block@=
Chunk chunk;
@

@<relations between object@=
List blocks_of_chunk(Chunk chk)
{
    return chk->blocks;
}
void block_extend_chunk(Block blk, Chunk chk)
{
    List blks = blocks_of_chunk(chk);
    blks = cons(blk, blks);
    chk->blocks = blks;
    blk->chunk = chk;
}
@

The contents of a block can either be a reference to the original text
or a reference to a chunk.

@<datatypes@=
typedef struct {
    enum {
        BLOCK_CONTENT_TEXT,
        BLOCK_CONTENT_CHUNK,
    } type;
    union {
        TextRef text;
        ChunkRef chunk;
    } value;
} *BlockContent;
@

A block contains a list of contents defined before.

@<mappings from block@=
List contents;
@

@<relations between object@=
List contents_of_block(Block blk)
{
    return blk->contents;
}

void block_contain_text(Block blk, TextRef tr)
{
    List bcs = contents_of_block(blk);
    BlockContent bc = NULL;

    NEW0(bc);
    bc->type = BLOCK_CONTENT_TEXT;
    bc->value.text = tr;

    bcs = cons(bc, bcs);
    blk->contents = bcs;
}

void block_include_chunk(Block blk, ChunkRef cr)
{
    List bcs = contents_of_block(blk);
    BlockContent bc = NULL;

    NEW0(bc);
    bc->type = BLOCK_CONTENT_CHUNK;
    bc->value.chunk = cr;

    bcs = cons(bc, bcs);
    blk->contents = bcs;
}
@

Sometimes it's useful to find the chunk the block extends.

@<relations between object@=
Chunk chunk_extended_by(Block blk)
{
    return blk->chunk;
}
@

A reference to chunk is consists of a chunk and a list of filters.

@<mappings from chunk ref@=
Chunk chunk;
List filters;
@

As said before, a filter is just a chunk.

@<relations between object@=
List filters_of_chunk_ref(ChunkRef ckr)
{
    return ckr->filters;
}

void chunk_ref_reference_to(ChunkRef ckr, Chunk chk)
{
    ckr->chunk = chk;
}

void chunk_ref_is_transformed_by(ChunkRef ckr, Chunk chk)
{
    List flts = filters_of_chunk_ref(ckr);
    flts = cons(chk, flts);
    ckr->filters = flts;
}

Chunk chunk_of_chunk_ref(ChunkRef ckr)
{
    return ckr->chunk;
}
@

A litar archive is consists of comments and blocks, we call a pair of a comment and a block
a section.
@<datatypes@=
typedef struct {
    TextRef comment;
    Block block;
} *Section;
@

@<global variables@=
List sections;
@

The grammar of litar is designed deliberately to be as simple as possible.
As a result, the tokens only have four types, control character,
normal character, newline and EOF,
and the whole parsing process can be implemented as a finite state machine.

@<datatypes@=
typedef enum {
    @<states in a parsing@>
} State;

typedef struct {
    char *file;
    FILE *in;
    /* current position, current line, current row */
    int cp, cl, cr;
} *InputFrame;

typedef struct {
    enum {
        TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER,
        TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER,
        TOKEN_NEWLINE,
        TOKEN_EOF,
    } type;
    char value;
} *Token;
@

@<functions@=
static Token next_token(InputFrame ipt)
{
    Token tkn = NULL;
    char c;
    int n = 0;

    NEW0(tkn);

    n += (c = fgetc(ipt->in)) == EOF ? 0 : 1;
    if (c == '@') {
        tkn->type = TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER;
        n += (c = fgetc(ipt->in)) == EOF ? 0 : 1;
        if (c == EOF || c == '\n' || c == '\t' || c == ' ') {
            tkn->value = ' ';
        } else {
            tkn->value = c;
        }
        if (c == '\n') {
            n -= ungetc(c, ipt->in) == EOF ? 0 : 1;
        }
    } else if (c == '\n') {
        tkn->type = TOKEN_NEWLINE;
        tkn->value = '\n';
        ipt->cp += n;
        ++ipt->cl;
        ipt->cr = 0;
        n = 0;
    } else if (c == EOF) {
        tkn->type = TOKEN_EOF;
        tkn->value = EOF;
    } else {
        tkn->type = TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER;
        tkn->value = c;
    }
    ipt->cp += n;
    ipt->cr += n;

    return tkn;
}
@

The parsing start with the state reading in comments.

@<states in a parsing@=
COMMENT,
@

@<parse input@=
do {
    State cs = COMMENT; /* current state */
    List stack = empty_list;

    @<context for parsing@>

    do {
        InputFrame ipt = NULL;
        NEW0(ipt);
        ipt->file = strdup(archive_name);
        push(ipt, &stack);
    } while (0);

    while (!is_empty_list(stack)) {
        Token tkn;
        InputFrame ipt;

        RENAME(car(stack), ipt);

        if (ipt->cp == 0) {
            if ((ipt->in = fopen(ipt->file, "r")) == NULL) {
                perror(ipt->file);
                return 1;
            }
        }

        int cp_t, cl_t, cr_t;

        cp_t = ipt->cp;
        cl_t = ipt->cl;
        cr_t = ipt->cr;

        tkn = next_token(ipt);
        switch (cs) {
        @<cases for handling token "tkn"@>
        }

        FREE(tkn);
    }
} while (0);
@

@<functions@=
void handle_unknown_token(Token tkn, char *f, int l, int r)
{
    fprintf(stderr,
        "Unexpected token at line %d row %d of %s\n",
        l, r, f
    );
    exit(1);
}
@

@<execute token handlings@=
echo "do {"
cat -
echo "handle_unknown_token(tkn, ipt->file, cl_t, cr_t);"
echo "} while (0);"
@

@<cases for handling token "tkn"@=
case COMMENT:
    @<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "COMMENT"@|execute token handlings@>
    break;
@

@<context for parsing@=
TextRef ctxt = NULL;
Section csec = NULL;

NEW0(ctxt);
ctxt->file = archive_name;

NEW0(csec);
csec->comment = ctxt;
csec->block = NULL;
push(csec, &sections);
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "COMMENT"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
    ctxt->end = ipt->cp;
    break;
}
@

End parsing of currnet file.
@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "COMMENT"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_EOF) {
    fclose(ipt->in);
    free(ipt->file);
    FREE(ipt);
    pop(&stack);
    break;
}
@

@<states in a parsing@=
BLOCK_CHUNK_NAME,
BLOCK_CONTENTS,
@

@<cases for handling token "tkn"@=
case BLOCK_CHUNK_NAME:
    @<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "BLOCK_CHUNK_NAME"@|execute token handlings@>
    break;
case BLOCK_CONTENTS:
    @<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "BLOCK_CONTENTS"@|execute token handlings@>
    break;
@

@<context for parsing@=
Block cblk = NULL;
Str cache = NULL;
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "COMMENT"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '<') {
    NEW0(cblk);
    new_str(&cache, "");
    csec->block = cblk;
    cs = BLOCK_CHUNK_NAME;
    break;
}
@

Read in chunk name.
@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "BLOCK_CHUNK_NAME"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
    str_append(cache, tkn->value);
    break;
}
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '=') {
    char *chkn;
    Chunk chk;

    chkn = raw_string(cache);
    chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
    block_extend_chunk(cblk, chk);
    
    free_str(&cache);

    ctxt = NULL;
    NEW0(ctxt);
    ctxt->file = strdup(ipt->file);
    ctxt->start = ipt->cp;
    ctxt->end = ipt->cp;

    block_contain_text(cblk, ctxt);

    cs = BLOCK_CONTENTS;
    break;
}
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "BLOCK_CONTENTS"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
    ++ctxt->end;
    break;
}
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == ' ') {
    cblk = NULL;

    ctxt = NULL;
    NEW0(ctxt);
    ctxt->file = strdup(ipt->file);
    ctxt->start = cp_t;
    ctxt->end = cp_t;

    csec = NULL;
    NEW0(csec);
    csec->comment = ctxt;
    csec->block = NULL;
    push(csec, &sections);
    cs = COMMENT;
    break;
}
@

@<states in a parsing@=
REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME,
@

@<context for parsing@=
ChunkRef cckr = NULL;
@

@<cases for handling token "tkn"@=
case REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME:
    @<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME"@|execute token handlings@>
    break;
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "BLOCK_CONTENTS"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '<') {
    new_str(&cache, "");
    NEW0(cckr);
    block_include_chunk(cblk, cckr);
    cs = REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME;
    break;
}
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
    str_append(cache, tkn->value);
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '>') {
    char *chkn;
    Chunk chk;

    chkn = raw_string(cache);
    chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
    free_str(&cache);

    chunk_ref_reference_to(cckr, chk);
    cckr = NULL;
    cs = BLOCK_CONTENTS;
    break;
}
@

@<states in a parsing@=
REFERENCE_FILTER,
@

@<cases for handling token "tkn"@=
case REFERENCE_FILTER:
    @<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "REFERENCE_FILTER"@|execute token handlings@>
    break;
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '|') {
    char *chkn;
    Chunk chk;

    chkn = raw_string(cache);
    chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
    free_str(&cache);

    chunk_ref_reference_to(cckr, chk);

    new_str(&cache, "");
    cs = REFERENCE_FILTER;
    break;
}
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "REFERENCE_FILTER"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '|') {
    if (cache != NULL) {
        char *chkn;
        Chunk chk;

        chkn = raw_string(cache);
        chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
        free_str(&cache);

        chunk_ref_is_transformed_by(cckr, chk);
    }
    new_str(&cache, "");
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
    str_append(cache, tkn->value);
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '>') {
    char *chkn;
    Chunk chk;

    chkn = raw_string(cache);
    chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
    free_str(&cache);

    chunk_ref_is_transformed_by(cckr, chk);
    cs = BLOCK_CONTENTS;
    break;
}
@

@<command line options for litar@=
["Print the content of CHUNK in ARCHIVE.", "p", "CHUNK"],
["Execute an operation in CHUNK under ARCHIVE.", "x", "CHUNK"],
@

@<cases for command line option@=
case 'p':
    break;
@

@<operations after parsing input@=
@<print all sections@>
@

@<functions@=
void print_section(Section sec)
{
    printf("Comment: (%s, %d, %d)\n",
        sec->comment->file,
        sec->comment->start,
        sec->comment->end
    );
    if (sec->block) {
        printf("Block: %s\n", name_of_chunk(sec->block->chunk));
        List lst = copy_list(contents_of_block(sec->block));
        reverse(&lst);
        do {
            BlockContent bc;
            FOREACH(bc, lst) {
                if (bc->type == BLOCK_CONTENT_TEXT) {
                    printf("\tText: (%s, %d, %d)\n",
                        bc->value.text->file,
                        bc->value.text->start,
                        bc->value.text->end
                    );
                } else {
                    printf("\tReference: %s", name_of_chunk(bc->value.chunk->chunk));
                    List lst2 = copy_list(bc->value.chunk->filters);
                    reverse(&lst2);
                    Chunk chk;
                    FOREACH(chk, lst2) {
                        printf(" | %s", name_of_chunk(chk));
                    }
                    free_list(&lst2);
                    putchar('\n');
                }
            }
        } while (0);
        free_list(&lst);
    }
}
@

@<print all sections@=
do {
    Section sec;
    reverse(&sections);
    FOREACH(sec, sections) {
        print_section(sec);
    }
} while (0);
@

@<includes@=
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <ctype.h>

#include "light.h"
#include "str.h"
#include "list.h"
#include "hash_table.h"
#include "atom.h"
#include "deque.h"
@

@<parse invocation syntax@=
import json
import sys
from typing import NamedTuple

class Option(NamedTuple):
    name: str
    argument: str
    comment: str

stx = json.load(sys.stdin)

name = stx["name"]
options = list(map(
    lambda opt: Option(opt[1], opt[2] if len(opt)==3 else None, opt[0]),
    stx["options"]
))
arguments = stx["arguments"]
@

@<synopsis for manpage@=
python -c '
@<parse invocation syntax@>
str = f".B {name}\n"

def opt_to_str(opt):
    if opt.argument is not None:
        return f"\\fB\\-{opt[0]}\\fR \\fI{opt[1]}\\fR"
    else:
        return f"\\fB\\-{opt[0]}\\fR"

str += "[" + "|".join(map(opt_to_str, options)) + "]"
for arg in arguments:
    str += f" \\fI{arg}\\fR"
print(str)
'
@

@<options for manpage@=
python -c '
@<parse invocation syntax@>
str = ""

def opt_to_str(opt):
    if opt.argument is None:
        return f".BR \\-{opt.name}\n"
    else:
        return f".BR \\-{opt.name} \" \" \\fI{opt.argument}\\fR\n"

for opt in options:
    str += ".TP\n"
    str += opt_to_str(opt)
    str += opt.comment + "\n"

print(str)
'
@

@<usage of invocation syntax@=
python -c '
@<parse invocation syntax@>
str = "\""
str += name

def opt_to_str(opt):
    if opt.argument is not None:
        return f"-{opt[0]} {opt[1]}"
    else:
        return f"-{opt[0]}"

str += " [" + "|".join(map(opt_to_str, options)) + "]"

for arg in arguments:
    str += " " + arg
str += "\""

print(str, end="")
'
@

@<getopt string for invocation syntax@=
python -c '
@<parse invocation syntax@>

def opt_to_str(opt):
    if opt.argument is None:
        return opt.name
    else:
        return opt.name + ":"

print("\"" + "".join(map(opt_to_str, options)) + "\"", end="")
'
@

@<fill in build time information@=
sed -e "s/<build time>/$(date -Iminute)/g"
@

@<execute sequentially@=
echo "do {"
cat -
echo "} while (0);"
@

@<print@=
echo 'printf('
sed 's/\\/\\\\/g' | sed 's/"/\\"/g' | sed 's/^/"/' | sed 's/$/\\n"/'
echo ');'
@

@<remove ending comma@=
sed -z 's/,[[:space:]]$/\n/'
@


